    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Assignment3_Verify the Claim: Philadelphia Poverty Change\"\nsubtitle: \"Testing if ACS data supports the 2018 news story\"\nauthor: Gautami Kasat\ndate: today\nformat:\n  html:\n    toc: true\n    code-fold: true\n    code-summary: \"View Code\"\n    embed-resources: true\nexecute:\n  warning: false\n  message: false\n---\n\n## The Enquiry\n\nThis report tests whether the ACS data supports the claims in Alfred Lubrano's 2018 article, [\"New Census figures on Philly neighborhoods show inequality, high numbers of whites living in poverty\"](https://whyy.org/articles/new-census-figures-on-philly-neighborhoods-show-inequality-high-numbers-of-whites-living-in-poverty/) (WHYY / Philadelphia Inquirer, December 6, 2018). \n\n## Setup\n\n\n\n## Part 1: Reconstruct\n\n### Variables \nI pulled four ACS variables. For overall poverty, B17001_002 counts people below the poverty level in the past twelve months, and B17001_001 gives the population for whom poverty status is determined. Because several claims are specifically about white poverty, I also pulled B17001A_001 and B17001A_002, the same table restricted to people identifying as white alone. ACS publishes counts, not rates, so both the rate and its margin of error have to be calculated. I used the 2006-2010 and 2013-2017 five-year estimates at the census tract level. Five-year estimates are the only ones that reach tracts; one-year estimates cover only areas of 65,000 people or more.\n\n### Assumptions \nThe article does not document its method, so several choices are mine. It does not name the ACS table it used; I assumed B17001. It says \"white\" without defining it, so I used B17001A, white alone, which includes people who also identify as Hispanic. And it reports neighborhood figures, which are not Census geographies: its reporters aggregated tracts into boundaries of their own choosing and never published which tracts went where, so my numbers will not match theirs. Every figure in the article is a point estimate, with no margins of error given. \n\n### Overlap\nThe two periods do not overlap. Census Bureau guidance is explicit that only non-overlapping five-year estimates should be compared over time, so on this point the article is on solid ground.\n\n### Pulling the ACS data: \n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\npov_2010 <- get_acs(\n  geography = \"tract\",\n  variables = c( pov_total = \"B17001_001\",pov_poor = \"B17001_002\", wht_total = \"B17001A_001\",wht_poor = \"B17001A_002\"),\n  state = \"PA\",\n  county = \"Philadelphia\",\n  year = 2010,\n  output = \"wide\",\n  survey = \"acs5\"\n)\n\npov_2017 <- get_acs(\n  geography = \"tract\",\n  variables = c( pov_total = \"B17001_001\",pov_poor = \"B17001_002\", wht_total = \"B17001A_001\",wht_poor = \"B17001A_002\"),\n  state = \"PA\",\n  county = \"Philadelphia\",\n  year = 2017,\n  output = \"wide\",\n  geometry = TRUE,\n  survey = \"acs5\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n\n  |                                                                            \n  |                                                                      |   0%\n  |                                                                            \n  |=                                                                     |   1%\n  |                                                                            \n  |=                                                                     |   2%\n  |                                                                            \n  |==                                                                    |   2%\n  |                                                                            \n  |==                                                                    |   3%\n  |                                                                            \n  |===                                                                   |   4%\n  |                                